<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis I of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a>–<a href="/years/1547">1547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as François Ier, Francis I · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis I was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1515 until his death in 1547, the son of Charles, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, and Louise of Savoy, and the successor of his father-in-law Louis XII. A patron of the arts, he drew Italian artists to France, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>, and promoted the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-language">French language</a>. His reign saw the growth of central power, the spread of humanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, and the voyages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. He continued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> and afterwards formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-ottoman-alliance">Franco-Ottoman alliance</a> with the Ottoman sultan Suleiman.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france.jpg" alt="Francis I of France" width="330" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Clouet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:François_Ier_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francis was born on 12 September 1494 at the Château de Cognac, the only son of Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>. His family was not expected to inherit the throne, but King Charles VIII died childless in 1498 and Louis XII had no male heir. Because the Salic Law barred women from the succession, the four-year-old Francis became heir presumptive and Duke of Valois. He married Louis's daughter Claude on 18 May 1514. Louis died on 1 January 1515, and Francis was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 25 January.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> dominated the reign, and Francis led his troops in person. He routed the Papal States and the Old Swiss Confederacy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Marignano</a> on 13–15 September 1515 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, but failed to win the imperial election of 1519 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. His meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> at the Field of Cloth of Gold on 7 June 1520 produced no agreement.</p><p>At Pavia on 24 February 1525 he was taken prisoner and held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. He wrote to his mother, &quot;Of all things, nothing remains to me but honour and life, which is safe.&quot; The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1526">Treaty of Madrid</a> of 1526 freed him in exchange for his two sons, but he repudiated it once back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>Francis financed the building of Chambord, rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, and employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>, Benvenuto Cellini and Primaticcio. He made Guillaume Budé chief librarian and by the Ordonnance de Montpellier of 1537 required a copy of every book sold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for his library. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordinance-of-villers-cotterets">Ordinance of Villers-Cotterêts</a> of 1539 made French the administrative language of the kingdom and ordered parish registers of births, marriages and deaths.</p><p>He sent Giovanni da Verrazzano to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1524 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a> up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> in 1534, and made an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> that contemporaries called the impious alliance. After the Affair of the Placards on 17 October 1534 he began to persecute Protestants, and in 1545 ordered the Massacre of Mérindol of the Waldensians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Francis died at the Château de Rambouillet on 31 March 1547 and was succeeded by his son Henry II. His tomb at Saint Denis was desecrated on 20 October 1793 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. His reputation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> is poor: he is remembered as the king defeated and captured at Pavia, and the historian Jules Michelet set the negative image. The British historian Glenn Richardson judges him a success, and calls his cultural legacy to France immense.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francis I of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eitel Friedrich II took part in the wars of Maximilian I, and his son Eitel Friedrich III died at Pavia in 1525, during the Italian campaign in which the imperial army defeated and captured <b>Francis I of France</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Swiss pikemen restored the Sforza in 1512 and won at Novara in 1513, but <b>Francis I of France</b> defeated them at the Battle of Marignano in 1515.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Weak in the fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, Savoy suffered its worst disaster in 1536, when <b>Francis I of France</b> occupied Savoy and most of Piedmont, Bern conquered the Vaud and Geneva established its independence under the Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles V’s armies defeated and captured <b>Francis I of France</b> at the Battle of Pavia in 1525, sacked Rome in 1527, took Tunis in 1535 and crushed the German Protestant princes at Mühlberg in 1547.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Italian Wars from 1494 French kings fought Spain and the Habsburgs, winning at the Battle of Marignano in 1515 but losing <b>Francis I</b> as a prisoner at the Battle of Pavia in 1525.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Italian Wars the Confederates briefly controlled the Duchy of Milan and won the Battle of Novara in 1513, but their defeat by <b>Francis I of France</b> at the Battle of Marignano in 1515 ended their ambitions as a great power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
